    <p>
      BMS looks like a small door even for students with maths. In the part of the seat matrix we could read, it appears at four colleges: Aryabhatta (104 seats across categories), College of Vocational Studies (60), Deen Dayal Upadhyaya (60) and Keshav Mahavidyalaya (59).
    </p>
    <p>
      That is 283 seats, against 3,651 B.Com (Hons) seats in 27 colleges. If you hold no maths, the door that closed is a narrow one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="five-slots">One five-test set for three DU lines</h2>

  <p>
    Here is a short piece of arithmetic that no ranking page shows. Take a language, Accountancy, Business Studies, Economics and the General Aptitude Test. That is five tests.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>B.Com (Hons), Combination II: language + Accountancy + two List B subjects (Business Studies and Economics). Valid.</li>
    <li>B.Com (General), Combination I: language + any three List B subjects (Accountancy, Business Studies, Economics). Valid.</li>
    <li>B.Com (General), Combination II: language + one List B subject + General Aptitude Test. Valid.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    One set of five tests reaches three DU lines, and merit is based on the best score from any valid combination. Whether the General Aptitude Test counts inside a five-test cap depends on NTA's 2027 bulletin. CUET 2026 allowed up to five subjects, so check the 2027 notice before you register.
  </p>

