  <h2 id="drop">Gap year or settle: the arithmetic</h2>

  <p>
    A gap year feels like one year. In time-to-income terms it is bigger. A three-year B.Com plus one gap year means four years before degree-based pay, which is 33% longer.
  </p>

  <p>
    That cost can be worth it, and sometimes it is not. A useful test has three parts:
  </p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    <li><strong>A specific target.</strong> Name the college and course, and look up its last two cutoff lists. "A better college" is not a target.</li>
    <li><strong>A realistic gain.</strong> Compare your own best CUET combination score with the target's cutoff. If the gap is the kind a year of focused practice can close, a retake can pay. If it is a gap like the one between a top college and the middle of the list, a year may not move you far.</li>
    <li><strong>A parallel track.</strong> The year should also produce a registration, a skill or a project. A year of only waiting is the expensive version.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The seat data says mid-ranked B.Com (Hons) seats do fill, and many go late. If your gap is small, joining a good-fit college and building proof from year one usually beats a drop. Reserve the gap year for a clear target and a clear plan.
    </p>
  </div>

